      "notes": "declared equivalent: remove_branch_force_false on line 27 (`if \"..\" in components:`) forces this branch to never be taken. Tracing control flow in the reference: any name containing '/' or '\\\\' is already rejected earlier (line 21), so by the time line 26 runs (`components = name.replace(\"\\\\\", \"/\").split(\"/\")`), name contains neither separator and components is always exactly [name] (a single-element list). Therefore `\"..\" in components` is equivalent to `name == \"..\"`, which was already rejected earlier still (line 23's `name in (\".\", \"..\")` check). The condition at line 27 is provably always False for every input that reaches it, on any input the task contract cares about -- it is dead code given the current (correct) check ordering, so forcing it to never fire changes nothing. Confirmed empirically: the sibling remove_branch_force_true mutant at the same line (which forces the branch to always fire) IS correctly killed by the hidden suite (breaks plain names), and invert_comparison at the same line is also correctly killed -- both because they change behavior on inputs that DO reach line 27, unlike force_false/remove_validation which only affect the (empty) set of inputs for which the guard would have been True."
    },
